data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work without any unsafe context or special directives:

```csharp
Span<nint>  signed   = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> unsigned = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit runtime, each element is 8 bytes (matching pointer size). The only caveat is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context since the size isn't fixed at compile time — use `IntPtr.Size` instead.
